C N O R}_{x}]$ was varied in the series ${\mathsf{T C N O F}}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{T C N Q C l}}_{2},$ $\mathsf{T C N Q B r}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{T C N O M e}_{2}$ , and ${\mathsf{T C N Q}}({\mathsf{M e O}})_{2},$ , in addition to TCNQ and ${\mathsf{T C N O F}}_{4}$ . However, all materials, except for $\mathsf{T C N O F}_{4}.$ , are virtually neutral.23
An Ionicity Diagram for Systematic Investigation of CT in the $\begin{array}{r}{{[{\tt R u}_{2}}^{{\tt I I},{\tt I I}}]}\end{array}$ /TCNQ, DCNQI System
The ionicity diagram made by the plot of $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})$ vs $\Delta E_{1/2}\mathrm{^{-}}$ (DA) could be basically extended to all of the D/A family synthesized by the reaction of $\mathrm{|{Ru}}_{2}^{\mathrm{{II},\mathrm{{II}}}}({\mathrm{R}}^{\prime}{\mathrm{CO}}_{2})_{4}(\mathrm{{THF}})_{2}]$ and $\mathsf{T C N Q R}_{x}$ or $\mathsf{D C N Q I R}_{x},$ because the validity of this ionicity diagram is essentially only dependent on the accuracy of the DFT calculations. $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})$ was plotted as a function of $\Delta E_{1/2}\mathbf{\bar{\Sigma}}$ (DA) for the 13 materials reported up until now, as shown in Figure 5. The linear relation is certainly adopted in the expanded family even with a variety of D/A combinations. The crossover point of the least-squares linear fitted line with $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})=0$ is ${\approx}400~\mathrm{mV}$ , consistent with only the case of $\mathsf{\{R u_{2}(C F_{3}C O_{2})_{4}\}_{2}T C N O R_{x}]}$ (Figure 4a). As explained above, the D/A combinations in the lower left space with $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})$ $<0$ (blue and green squares in Figure 5) should be ionic, whereas those in the upper right space with $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathrm{DA})>0$ (red circle in Figure 5) are neutral. The actual charge distribution of all compounds is in good agreement with this classification. Very interestingly, a compound exhibiting an $\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{I}$ transition was found just in a combination at the $\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{I}$ boundary (purple triangle in Figure 5); this fact justifies the use of this ionicity diagram for the D/A family made from $\mathrm{[Ru}_{2}^{\mathrm{{II},\mathrm{{II}}}}(\mathrm{{R}^{\prime}{C O}_{2})_{4}(\mathrm{{THF})_{2}}]}$ and TCNQRx/DCNQIRx.
## ${\pmb{16}}^{-}$ Transferred $\bar{\mathbf{D}}_{2}\bar{\mathbf{A}}$ as Magnets
The interlayer interaction is very strong in this system on the whole, leading to the formation of 3-D magnetic ordering at a relatively high temperature. $\mathrm{\small{[\{Ru_{2}(C F_{3}C O_{2})_{4}\}_{2}T C N O F_{4}]}}$ 3 3(toluene) shows antiferromagnetic order at $T_{\mathsf{N}}=95\mathsf{K}.^{21,22}$ It should be noted that this is not a simple antiferromagnet, but a field-induced ferrimagnet with a remnant magnetization of ${\approx}36\%$ and a large coercivity with $H_{\mathrm{c}}\approx2.32\tau$ . This interesting behavior should be due to the strong anisotropic nature of $\left[\mathsf{R u}_{2}^{\mathsf{I I},\mathsf{I I}}\right]$ $(D_{S=1}\approx250\mathrm{-}300\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ and $\mathsf{\{R u}}_{2}^{\mathsf{\tiny{I I,I I I}}}\mathsf{J}^{+}$ $(D_{S=3/2}{\approx}60{-}70\mathrm{cm}^{-1}),{\l}^{9,29}$ even if they have facial anisotropy, and low structural dimensionality as 2-D layered materials. Indeed, this situation also causes a spin-canting state reflecting the interlayer environment associated with the symmetry between layers, the location of interstitial solvents, disorder of structures, structural defects, and so on. $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{\{Ru_{2}(\mathrm{o-}F P h C O_{2})_{4}}}\}.$ 2BTDA-TCNQ] (BTDA-TCNQ $\c=$ bis[1,2,5]- thiadizolotetracyanoquinodimethane) shows antiferromagnetic order at $T_{\mathsf{N}}=93\mathsf{K}$ followed by a spin-canting order at $T_{\mathsf{c}1}=87~\mathsf{K}$ and a rearrangement at $T_{\mathrm{c}2}=13~\mathsf{K},^{30}$ whereas $\mathrm{\Delta[\{Ru_{2}(p–F P h C O_{2})_{4}\}_{2}B T D A\ –T C N Q}$ ] shows a $3{-}D$ ferrimagnet order at $T_{\mathrm{c}}=83\ \mathsf{K}.$ Thus, the interlayer environment in the layered ${\tt D}_{2}{\tt A}$ compounds is crucial in deciding the state of the magnets. An intriguing example is $\mathrm{\small{[\{Ru_{2}({o\cdot}C l P h C O_{2})_{4}\}_{2}T C N Q\cdot}}$ $(\mathsf{M e O})_{2}\mathsf{l}\cdot\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2},$ which reversibly changes its magnetic behavior between antiferromagnet with $\begin{array}{r}{T_{\mathsf{N}}=}\end{array}$ $75~\mathsf{K}$ and ferrimagnet with $T_{\mathrm{c}}=56~\mathsf{K}$ by solvation/ desolvation of $C H_{2}C l_{2}$ located between the layers, although the only discernible difference is ordering/ disordering of a pendant ligand orientation without crucial change of the layer's lattice in the solvation/ desolvation process.31
The ${\sf D}_{2}{\sf A}$ type can also form a $3{-}D$ lattice (Figure 3), in which the magnetism is only dependent on the exchange coupling through the lattice. While $\{\{\mathsf{R u}_{2}(x\mathrm{-FPhCO}_{2})_{4}\}_{2}-$ BTDA-TCNQ] with $X^{-}=o r t h o^{-}$ and para- have the aforementioned 2-D lattices,30 the compound with $X^{-}=m e t a^{-}$ has a 3-D lattice in the same ${\sf D}_{2}{\sf A}$ type.32 Consequently, this $3{-}D$ material simply produces a ferrimagnetic order at $T_{\mathrm{c}}=107\:\mathsf{K}$ This $T_{\mathrm{c}},$ interestingly, decreases with increasing pressure.33 This means that rather than the effect on the bond distance, the effect of pressure on the bond angle of Ru N C is crucial in this compound, probably reducing the overlap integral for the superexchange.
## $2\bullet^{-}$ Transferred ${\pmb D}_{2}{\pmb A}$ with the Diamagnetic $\mathsf{T C N O}^{2-}$
Choosing an adequate combination of $\left[\mathsf{R u}_{2}^{\mathsf{I I},\mathsf{I I}}\right]$ and $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x},$ the $2\ensuremath{\mathbf{e}}^{-}$ transferred state in the ${\sf D}_{2}{\sf A}$ system with a formal state of $[\{{\mathsf{R}}{\mathsf{U}}_{2}{}^{[1,\mathsf{I I I}}\}^{+}-({\mathsf{T C N Q R}}_{x}^{2-})-\{{\mathsf{R}}{\mathsf{U}}_{2}{}^{[1,\mathsf{I I I}}\}^{+}]$ can be isolated. However, in this case, the $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})$ must be very large to overcome the on-site Coulomb repulsion on $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}^{2-}$ ; $|^{2}E_{1/2}(\mathsf{A})-$ $^1E_{1/2}(\mathsf{A})|=500-600\mathrm{\mV}$ for $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}$ . The combination of $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{R u}_{2}^{\mathsf{I I},\mathsf{I I}}(m\cdot\mathsf{M e P h C O}_{2})_{4}]$ $(m{\cdot}\mathbf{MePhCO}_{2}^{-}=m$ -methylbenzoate) and BTDA-TCNQ led to the formation of $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ transferred ${\sf D}_{2}{\sf A}$ in a $3{-}D$ infinite network.34 The important aspect is that this D/A combination has the largest negative $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathrm{DA})\left(\approx-0.6\mathrm{eV}\right)$ and the smallest $\Delta E_{1/2}(\mathsf{D A})$ $(\approx110\mathrm{\mV})$ among the 13 materials in Figure 5. Figure 5 was modified into a plot as a function of $|^{2}E_{1/2}(\mathsf{A})-{}^{1}E_{1/2}(\mathsf{A})|$ (Figure 7a), in which the onsite Coulomb repulsion of the respective $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}$ units is different, and we noticed that $\mathsf{T C N Q}(\mathsf{M e O})_{2}$ is the smallest among the compounds. The plot of the $\mathsf{L U M O_{A}}$ level vs $|^{2}E_{1/2^{-}}$ (A)  $^1E_{1/2}(\mathsf{A})|$ roughly shows a linear relation (Figure 7b), and then the least-squares line was inserted into Figure 7a only fixing the slope based on the value of $\mathfrak{l}\{\mathsf{R u}_{2}^{\mathsf{u},\mathsf{m}}(m\cdot$ $\mathsf{M e P h C O}_{2}\vert_{4}\}_{2}$ (BTDA-TCNQ)]. Thus, we suppose that materials having lower $\Delta E_{\mathrm{H-L}}(\mathsf{D A})$ values than the inserted line are candidates for the $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{e}}^{-}$ transferred system (green region in Figure 7a). Finally, a note about its magnetic properties: because $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}^{2}\mathrm{~_-~}$ is diamagnetic, $\mathrm{[\{Ru_{2}^{\scriptscriptstyle{11,111}}(m\mathrm{-}M e P h C O_{2})_{4}\}_{2}^{\scriptscriptstyle{-}}}$ (BTDA-TCNQ)] shows an isolated paramagnetic behavior with $S=3/2$ arising from two $\mathsf{|R u}_{2}^{\mathsf{I I I I}}(m\cdot\mathsf{M e P h C O}_{2})_{4}]^{+}$ units.34
## N I Transition in Covalently Bonded DA Systems
One of the targets of this D/A system is to find materials that exhibit the $\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{I}$ transition driven by a perturbation of external parameters such as temperature, pressure, and photoirradiation. Organic DA systems are the pioneers for such
N I transition materials,35 and the best-known material is a tetrathiafulvalene $p$ -chloranil compound (TTF-CA) discovered by Torrance and co-workers in 1981.24,36 Because organic $\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{I}$ transition materials have been found in the vicinity of the valley of Torrance's $\mathsf{v}$ -shaped diagram,24 the neutral D/A combinations located at around $\Delta E_{1/2}(\mathrm{DA})\approx400\:\mathrm{mV}$ (i.e., the Madelung potential) in Figure 5 possibly exhibit the N I transition. In fact, the DA chain compound, $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{R u}_{2}(2,3,5,6\mathsf{-F}_{4}\mathsf{P h C O}_{2})_{4}-$ (DMDCNQI)] $2(p$ -xylene) $(2,3,5,6\mathrm{-}\mathsf{F}_{4}\mathsf{P h C O}_{2}^{-}=2,3,5,6$ 6-tetrafluorobenzoate), which exhibited a temperature-induced $\mathsf{N}{-}\mathsf{I}$ transition, was obtained (purple triangle in Figure 5).27
For the A unit, we can see the clear distinction between the quinonoid structure for the N state and quasi-benzenoid structure for the I state (Figure 8d). Neutral DMDCNQI has a quinonoid structure with characteristic single $(b$ and $d$ in Figure 8d), double $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{C}=\mathsf{C})$ c and e), and triple $\textstyle(\mathsf{C}\equiv\mathsf{N};a)$ bonds, whereas anionic DMDCNQI (i.e., DMDCNQI•) has a nearly benzenoid structure with an unpaired electron delocalized over the entire complex, causing an averaging of the bond lengths. In the process of changing from N to I, the multiple bonds of $a,\ c,$ and $e$ should become longer, whereas the single bonds of $b$ and $d$ became shorter. Meanwhile, bond $f,$ which is not involved in the resonance scheme, could not be affected by this alternation. In fact, as shown in Figure 8b, these variations occur at $T_{1}$ (ca. 270 K) and $T_{2}$ (ca. 210 K) in a stepwise manner upon cooling.27
Thus, the charge $\delta$ on $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}^{\delta-}$ or $\mathsf{D C N Q I R}_{x}^{\delta-}$ can be roughly estimated from the structure using the Kistenmacher relationship37 $\delta=-\{A[c/(b+d)]+B\}$ , where the constants A and $B$ are decided by assuming a completely neutral and a fully $1\mathrm{e}^{-}$ ionized form of $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}/\mathsf{D C N O}|\mathsf{R}_{x}.$ For example, taking $\mathsf{T C N Q}^{038}$ and RbTCNQ39 as standard compounds for $\delta=$ 0 and 1, respectively, $A_{\mathrm{TCNQ}}{=}{-}41.667$ and $B_{\mathrm{TCNQ}}=19.833$ are obtained using the structural data reported previously, which are available for the $\mathsf{T C N O R}_{x}$ series, and taking $\begin{array}{r}{{[{\mathsf{R h}}_{2}}^{\mathsf{I I},\mathsf{I I}}({\mathsf{C F}}_{3}{\mathsf{C O}}_{2})_{4}-}\end{array}$ (DMDCNQI)] $(\delta=0)^{19}$ and $[\mathsf{M n}^{\mathrm{{III}}}(\mathsf{T M e s P})(\mathsf{D M D C N Q I})]$ ] $\mathrm{(TMesP=}$ meso-tetrakis(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)porphyrinate; $\delta=1$ ),40 they are $A_{\mathrm{DCNQI}}=-36.900$ and $B_{\mathrm{DCNOI}}=17.295$ , also available for the $\mathsf{D C N Q I R}_{x}$ series. Figure 8c shows a plot of the estimated $\delta$ values for this $\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{I}$ transition compound using the latter constants, and we confirm a two-step variation between N and I phases at $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ $(T>T_{1},$ N phase; $T_{1}>T>T_{2}$ , IM phase; $T<T_{2},$ I phase), consistent with the features of the $\left[\mathsf{R u}_{2}\right]$ unit (Figure 8a).27
This is the first example of an N I transition in a covalently bonded D/A system, and the CT between $\mathsf{D}^{0}\mathsf{A}^{0}$ and $\mathsf{D}^{+}\mathsf{A}^{-}$ can be well explained by the affinity of D and A, as proved by the ionicity diagram in Figure 5. Here, the biggest interest is this: what is the IM phase with a formal $\delta$ of ${\approx}0.5$ . The answer is simple, but a matter of vital importance: it is a mixture resulting from a homogeneous distribution of N and I chains (i.e., $50/50\%$ self-organized by responding to Coulombic interactions among adjacent chains (Figure 9).27 A Coulomb energy cost is necessary for two chains aligned in-phase to change simultaneously from N to I states, whereas chains aligned antiphase have a Coulomb energy gain to become I chains (Figure 9b). Below $T_{2}$ , the intrachain energy gain of the I state becomes large enough to overcome the Coulomb energy cost, allowing a uniform I state to form. This result is an experimental verification for the predictions of 30 years ago.4143 The most important aspect is the fact that Coulomb interactions, as well as the relation between $\boldsymbol{I}_{\mathrm{D}}$ of D and $E_{\mathsf{A}}$ of A used, control the longrange charge distribution in the solid state of homogeneous species.
## Summary and Outlook
In this Account, I have described our recent work on the rational design of D/A-MOFs and the control of CT in the frameworks. Our purpose in this work is rationally to design electronic/magnetic functional materials, in which both the local spins and hopping electrons synergistically interact. I have not made particular reference to electronic properties, such as electron transport properties, of materials in this paper, but the present D/A materials often provide interesting electronic conducting properties closely associated with CT states.22,27,34,44 As aforementioned, the CT materials based on transition metal complexes (i.e., D/A-MOFs) are literally a “reservoir of functions”, in which we can treat both the localized static spins and hopping and/or itinerant electrons in the same system via “controllable charge transfer”. In addition, the high redox activity of D/A-MOFs provides the high potential field in coordination space needed for the activation of inserted guest molecules; namely, it should be possible to create a unique system exhibiting lattice guest interactions. Such a system conjures up an image of multifunctional molecular systems where chemical reactions in pore and physical phenomena on lattices are synergistically invoked.
